Z||000000||Monkeyman||06-20-2001||06:19 AM||||Here's my stereo plan.  Someone tell me if this amp/sub will work.<P>Sony CDX 600 headunit (w/sub pre outs, if that matters)<P>JBL GT 5 1/4" separates<P>Sony XS L1035 10" sub<BR>     specs:40-150W RMS<BR>     88dB sens.<BR>     600W max<BR>     needs .7 cu ft (in a sealed enclosure)<P>Kenwood KAC-746 amp<BR>     specs (per Kenwood site):<BR>     Max. power output 70Wx4 (4<B>Q</B>)<BR>     35Wx4@4<B>Q</B>  14.4V RMS <BR>     50Wx4@1<B>Q</B>  14.4V RMS<P>I have absolutely no idea what the <B>Q</B> stands for.  Any ideas?  I'd like to drive my component speakers <I>and</I> my sub off of the same amp.  Can I do this?  The amp shows something about "bridging".  What does this mean and what will it accomplish?  <P>One other question.  I've heard that filling a sealed box with Poly-fil will trick the sub into thinking it's in a larger box.  True?  The box I'll be buying has a volume of .42 cu ft.  The sub specs recommend .7 cu ft.  Will Poly-fil make this work well?  Or do I need to search for a different sub?<P>Any help will be greatly appreciated.<P>MM||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||06-20-2001||07:53 AM||||Dave, the "Q" stands for ohms, a measurement for resistance. I don't have time, someone else will explain, I gotta' run.||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||06-20-2001||09:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>Here's my stereo plan.  Someone tell me if this amp/sub will work.<P>Sony CDX 600 headunit (w/sub pre outs, if that matters)<P>JBL GT 5 1/4" separates<P>Sony XS L1035 10" sub   &lt;-- I would try to find better sub then that (no offence)<BR>     specs:40-150W RMS<BR>     88dB sens.<BR>     600W max<BR>     needs .7 cu ft (in a sealed enclosure)<P>Kenwood KAC-746 amp &lt;-- I had one, not a bad amp<BR>     specs (per Kenwood site):<BR>     Max. power output 70Wx4 (4[b]Q</B>)<BR>     35Wx4@4<B>Q</B>  14.4V RMS <BR>     50Wx4@1<B>Q</B>  14.4V RMS<P>I have absolutely no idea what the <B>Q</B> stands for.  Any ideas?   Ohms, cheak out <A HREF="http://www.eatel.net/~amptech/elecdisc/spkrmlti.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eatel.net/~amptech/elecdisc/spkrmlti.htm</A>   you might get a idea of what they are<P> I'd like to drive my component speakers <I>and</I> my sub off of the same amp.  Can I do this?  Yes, You have a 4 channel amp, channel A - hook your components up normal way (right speaker positve to right speaker positive, right speaker negative right speaker negative, left speaker...) then on channel B (what you will use for sub) it will say where to bridge the amp, connect the two wires to where it says bridged, then your spekaers are hooked up.  you will run channel A setero-full range, channel B mono-low pass filter, for imput you will have two sets of RCA cables, you will only need to run one set from your source and set the thing next to the RCA cables on amp to just A input, this way you dont have to run both cables - you should be set<P>The amp shows something about "bridging".  What does this mean and what will it accomplish?<BR>  <BR>bridging - combines both channels (like right side and left side) and makes them into 1 channel, this will give you more power (what you want for your sub) <P>One other question.  I've heard that filling a sealed box with Poly-fil will trick the sub into thinking it's in a larger box.  True? <BR>I have always used poly fill, i would but its up to you  not sure if it really does though, technically it should slow down the soundwaves to make it think its a bigger box but  i dotn know if it has been proven or anything<P> The box I'll be buying has a volume of .42 cu ft.  The sub specs recommend .7 cu ft.  Will Poly-fil make this work well? <P>I would use it<P> Or do I need to search for a different sub? <P>Sony subs are Okay but they are nothign great, they look nice but they dont sound the best/loudest   i think it would be a good choise in a Fiero due to the fiero not being so big.<P>Any help will be greatly appreciated.<P>I tryed too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR>MM[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000003||KRMFiero||06-20-2001||09:07 AM||||actually how many sets of pre amp outputs does it have? you might be better off running two sets of RCA's to the amp  for a better sound, try to find out, well i got to horry and get to my exam   l8r<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red) Caddy 4.5 Project<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>      <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||06-20-2001||10:06 AM||||i've always heard fiberfill changes the percieved volume by 10-20%. if thats so at best it would put you at about .46 cubic feet. i use it 'cause i'm always trying to cram a sub into some small area. every little bit (of airspace) helps.<P>were you getting one of aus' console boxes? i thought i remembered reading that. probaly best to get the one with the most airspace and use the storage space for sub area too. if i remember rght his largest air space combo gave you .65? then if i've got the numbers right on the polyfill you would have about .78 cubic feet. that ought to do ya.<P>keep in mind that i kind of pulled these numbers out of my head. it's stuff i've read on but not recently. i also did the math in my head and for me thats flirtin' with disaster.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||06-20-2001||10:28 AM||||The <B> Q </B> is actually an "omega" sign that means ohms. Most car speakers are 4 ohms and the amp is showing you can run it down to 1 ohms. It's used if you wanted to have more than 1 speaker off of the same channel.<BR>As for the sub, I would locate a different subwoofer. A speaker requiring .7 stuffed into a .42 box will have very tight bass. I would go with an Infinity, Polk Audio, or a Kenwood woofer. These would be better suited for the smaller box. The poly-fill adds about 20% of simulated internal volume and smooths out the bass response. I would add some to the box you are using.<BR>Hook up would be 1 JBL to channel 1 the other to channel 2. Then "bridge" channels 3 and 4. Bridging is combining 2 stereo channels into 1 mono channel of higher power. Hook this up to the subwoofer. The crossover doesn't need to be messed with since the Head unit has a subwoofer output and is controlled by it.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||06-20-2001||10:48 AM||||williamthorneiii had a used Polk db for sale fi a very good price. You may want to check if he's still got it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||06-20-2001||02:53 PM||||Stimpy,<P>Thanks for the paid programming.... er... I mean good word, but I no longer have the sub.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000008||Monkeyman||06-20-2001||04:54 PM||||Thanx for the info, guys.  I'll look into another sub.  Just a few more questions, please.  Does the cone material make any difference?  I've seen some subs (good name brands, but lower line of subs) that use what's essentially a paper cone and others that use some form of plastic (?).  I was always under the impression that paper was cheap and the plastic (or whatever material it is) was much better, not only for durability but also for sound.  What's the general consensus on this?  <P>If I fill my sub box (yes, I'll be using one of Aus' boxes) with Poly-fil, what's the minimum volume I should look for?  Any suggestions on brand/model?  I'm looking to spend less than $100 right now.  I'll upgrade my speakers at a later time, but I have some serious monetary constraints right now.<P>What difference does the sensitivity rating make?  I've seen some with a rating of around 85 dB and others with a rating as high as the mid 90s.  Will this really make a difference?<P>Thanx again for all the help.<P>MM ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||06-20-2001||07:04 PM||||Monkey Man, The sensitivity of a sub is really how well it uses it's power. Its also called a subs efficiency. Escentially, the higher the better. If you have 2 subs, each getting the same amount of power in the same size enclosure, one is rated 85 dB, the other is rated 88 dB, the one rated 88dB will escentially be twice as loud. A change of 3 dB is perceived as twice (or half, depending on direction of change) as loud with the same power. The higher the sensitivity, the louder the sub will be. It does make a noticable difference if you have heard them both, you will notice a difference.<P>The difference in cone material therory:<BR>Paper is used in cheaper sub because it IS cheap. Paper is also light weight. The light weight of the paper is a good thing, thats why its used so often. The lighter the cone, the fast it can move, and the faster it can move, the tighter, move accurate bass it can reproduce. It is not very durable, thats why it is not used on higher end, more expensive models. Sub cones are made of many different things. Kevlar (very strong), Carbon Fiber (excellent choice, durable and light), Paper, Aluminum (strong and light, excellent for sound quality and loudness), Titanium (VERY expensive, however they sound like no other sub Iv ever heard, I believe Eclipse is the only brand making Titanium subs now). I would suggest Carbon Fiber, or Aluminum. Paper is good, but not as durable and does have a tendancy to crack, and produce cone distortion. <P>One thing I always hear people say is that Poly-fill "trick's the sub into thinking its in a larger box" technically, thats incorrect. The sub knows that size box its in, becuase of the resistance to cone movment. There is no way to change this, without changeing the volume of air inside the box. While yes, poly fill does take up some air space itself, so it actually makes the box smaller. It slows down the sound waves so that, for one thing there is less noise box resensence noise heard, and second, since the sound waves are slowed because of it, it will be *percieved* as louder.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||06-20-2001||07:40 PM||||Hey, this is just a suggestion, but I would look into perhaps an Image Dynamic's IDQ series woofer, with Dual 4ohm Voice coils. Then, you can run the coils in parallel for a 2ohm woofer, so you will get the most power from your amp. Seeing as that amp is rather lower power, an IDQ woofer is very efficient and would make good use of the power. They can be had for about $110 new. They have excellent sound quality, and do get descently loud, but are not really loud if thats what your looking for. They do have great sound quality though. Cones are Carbon Fiber if I recall correctly.||||reg||1
