Z||000015||Monkeyman||06-21-2001||04:08 AM||||Thanx for the tips.  I'm not completely convinced about being better prices than Crutchfield, though.  I looked up the headunit I'm currently running and it's $399!!!  Crutchfield had it (last years model) for $369 and I paid $349 @ hhgregg.  I'll double check on some other prices.<P>Oreif--I had my eye on a Cerwin Vega sub while I was looking at Crutchfields site.  I know the brand name is good, but don't they have a paper cone?  Isn't paper for the cheap subs (i.e. lower quality)?  How long should a paper cone last before drying out and cracking?  BTW--I ordered a .6 cu ft box.  Seems like the selection of subs was greater with that one.<P>As far as volume vs quality, I'd like something that is crisp and clear, reasonably loud (not enough to make my ears bleed, but I do tend to crank the volume), and something with enough "thump" to change my heart rhythm.  Am I asking too much for around a hundred bucks?<P>You guys are a great help.  Please keep up the help.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||06-21-2001||10:26 AM||||Paper cones are not only for "cheap" subs. Out of the list I sent you, the Cerwin has the highest db of sensativity. Paper cones if made correctly can produce very nice sound while keeping the power requirements low. The key there is the power. If you are worried about the durability of a paper cone, then my next suggestion would be the Kenwood.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mach10||06-21-2001||03:43 PM||||Paper isn't chosen just for cost-effectiveness. It's used because it doesn't resonate like other materials. It's not really paper, but Spruce Wood Pulp, formed into the shape, I.E., Paper Mache  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>It has an advantage of being VERY light, good harmonics, and yes, reasonably cheap. The other materials generally add stiffness, so that you can hit them harder without distorting the cone, but you can and do get some interesting resonance effects. However, since the Sub will only be handling FQs below 100Hz, this usually isn't a problem. SWP cones do tend to be more efficient, but a rock-crushing Eclipse with the titanium/aluminum cone will beat it out 'cos you can dump 2000+ watts of power into it.<BR>||||reg||6
